<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=leyden-jar">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Energy and power</p><h1 class="enc-title">Leyden jar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1745">1745</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Leiden jar, Kleistian jar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leiden">Leyden</a> jar is an electrical component that stores a high-voltage charge between conductors on the inside and outside of a glass jar, and was the original form of the capacitor. It was discovered independently by the German cleric Ewald Georg von Kleist on 11 October 1745 and by Pieter van Musschenbroek of Leiden in 1745–1746, with Andreas Cunaeus the first to see why it needed to be held in the hand. It was the first means of accumulating electric charge in large quantities and discharging it at will. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benjamin-franklin">Benjamin Franklin</a> later linked jars in series, using the term electrical battery.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leyden-jar.jpg" alt="Leyden jar" width="120" height="175" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was WHell at German Wikipedia. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leid-flasch.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>11 October 1745</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Cammin, Pomerania (now Germany and Poland)</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Ewald Georg von Kleist · Pieter van Musschenbroek · Andreas Cunaeus</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Also arose independently</span>Leiden, Netherlands (1745–1746)</p><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Long credited to Musschenbroek, it was first made by von Kleist; Cunaeus first discovered why the jar had to be held in the hand.</p><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=leyden-jar">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
